TypeScript is a programming language developed by Microsoft that is a superset of JavaScript. It was created to help developers write more robust and maintainable JavaScript code by adding optional static typing, interfaces, classes, and other features. TypeScript adds a layer of safety and predictability to JavaScript, making it easier to catch errors and understand the structure of complex projects.

Duck typing, on the other hand, is a concept in object-oriented programming that allows an object to be used as if it belonging to a certain type, even if it doesn't have the exact properties or methods that are typically associated with that type. In other words, if an object "quacks like a duck" (has similar behavior) and "walks like a duck" (performs similar tasks), it can be treated as a duck.

In TypeScript, duck typing is a fundamental principle used when working with interfaces. Interfaces in TypeScript are used to define the shape of an object, including the properties and methods it should have. When an object is passed to a function that expects a certain interface, TypeScript will check if the object conforms to that interface. If the object has the required properties and methods, it will be considered compatible with the interface, and the function will be able to work with the object.

For example, consider the following interface in TypeScript:

```typescript
interface Bird {
  fly(): void;
  peck(): void;
}
```

Now, suppose you have an object `duck` that has both the required methods (fly and peck):

```typescript
const duck = { fly: () => {}, peck: () => {} };
```

You can pass `duck` to a function that expects a `Bird` interface without any issues, because `duck` satisfies the requirements of the interface:

```typescript
function takeBirdToPond(bird: Bird) {
  bird.fly();
  bird.peck();
}

takeBirdToPond(duck);
```

This way, TypeScript helps ensure that the function will work as intended with the provided object, because the object "quacks like a duck" in terms of its behavior.

In summary, TypeScript allows developers to write more robust and maintainable JavaScript code by adding optional static typing features, while duck typing enables the use of objects with similar behavior as a certain type without having to inherit from a specific class or interface. TypeScript leverages this concept through its use of interfaces, providing a more dynamic and flexible approach to typing in JavaScript.

Here's the code for a Discord.js v14 bot with a ban command using slash commands:

1. Install the necessary packages:
```bash
npm install discord.js
npm install @discordjs/rest
npm install dotenv
```
2. Create a `.env` file and add the following environment variables:

```bash
TOKEN=<your bot token>
APPLICATION_ID=<your bot application ID>
GUILD_ID=<your server ID>
```
Replace `<your bot token>`, `<your bot application ID>`, and `<your server ID>` with actual data. The bot token can be obtained from the Discord Developer Portal, while the application ID and server ID can be obtained from the Discord client under the "Developer" tab.

3. Create a file named `index.js`:
```javascript
const { Client, GatewayIntentBits, Partials } = require('discord.js');
const { REST } = require('@discordjs/rest');
const { Routes } = require('discord-api-types/v9');
const client = new Client({ intents: [GatewayIntentBits.Guilds, Partials.Message], partials: [Partials.Message] });

// Load the commands
client.commands = new Map();

fs.readdirSync('./commands').forEach((file) => {
  if (!file.endsWith('.js')) return;
  const command = require(`./commands/${file}`);
  client.commands.set(command.data.name, command);
});

// Register slash commands
const rest = new REST({ version: '9' }).setToken(process.env.TOKEN);
rest.put(Routes.applicationGuildCommands(process.env.APPLICATION_ID, process.env.GUILD_ID), { body: client.commands.map((command) => command.data.toJSON()) })
  .then(() => console.log('Slash commands registered.'))
  .catch(console.error);

// Listen for interactions
client.on('interactionCreate', async (interaction) => {
  if (!interaction.isCommand()) return;

  const command = client.commands.get(interaction.commandName);
  if (!command) return;

  try {
    await command.execute(interaction);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(error);
    await interaction.reply({ content: 'There was an error while executing this command!', ephemeral: true });
  }
});

// Login to Discord with your bot token
client.login(process.env.TOKEN);
```
Replace `./commands` with the actual path to your commands folder, which should contain each command as a separate file, following the Model–View–Controller (MVC) architecture.

Each command file should look like this:
```javascript
